{
 "_about": "Day 3, Task 2: hand-made answer key for 20 judgments (12 Supreme Court, 8 Lahore High Court; random sample, seed 20, of judgments with 1,200-5,000 words). Annotated by Claude (an AI model, not a person) from every sentence a deliberately loose search flagged (reporter-like year patterns, 'v.'/'case of', section/article/order/rule + number) and from the bench line of each PDF's first page, WITHOUT looking at the extractor's output. citations: law-report citations of precedents (incomplete ones, where the page number is cut off in the text, are left out). provisions: [act, provision] for every numbered provision of a named law that the judgment refers to, including 'the Code' / 'the Act' when the law is clear from context; sub-clauses are dropped (Section 302(b) -> Section 302). judges: the bench. Act ids are canonical short names; evaluations/graph_extraction_eval.py maps the extractor's Act nodes onto them. After the first scoring run, the 3 links the extractor found that were not in the key were checked against the full text: 2 were real and missed by the key (its candidate sentences were cut at 420 characters) and were added (SC_c.a._842_2024 Article 199; LHC_2026LHC8323 CPC Section 96); 1 stays wrong (Article 98 in SC_c.p._1106_2024 is from the 1962 Constitution, quoted inside a precedent). A second check after the extractor was improved found 2 more real links the key had missed (SC_c.a._842_2024: 'its section 4' of the Supreme Court (Practice and Procedure) Act; LHC_2026LHC8604: CPC section 47 inside the quoted text of section 145), now added. Four Indian AIR citations whose court abbreviation is garbled in the PDF text (A.B., Born., Dehly) are written as printed, since the true reporter abbreviation cannot be read from the document.",
